WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.666 --> 00:00:11.196
So Dave Robinson did a kind of
live analysis of this dog breed

00:00:11.196 --> 00:00:13.486
data, and he made a
Shiny app out of it.

00:00:13.486 --> 00:00:17.166
So maybe I can walk through
what it looks like in Py Shiny

00:00:17.166 --> 00:00:19.956
and just try to translate
some of that to Py Shiny.

00:00:19.956 --> 00:00:22.636
So I watched him
work with this data,

00:00:22.636 --> 00:00:24.306
so I know a little bit about it.

00:00:24.306 --> 00:00:25.926
And for some context,
I think there's

00:00:25.926 --> 00:00:28.925
a little bit of cleaning that
has to happen and then some

00:00:28.925 --> 00:00:29.796
reshaping.

00:00:29.796 --> 00:00:33.456
The interesting
thing is I understand

00:00:33.456 --> 00:00:35.946
there's three pieces of data.

00:00:35.946 --> 00:00:41.886
You have these breed traits, so
this is for each breed of dog.

00:00:41.886 --> 00:00:45.336
It's ranked or I think it's
rated on a number of things

00:00:45.336 --> 00:00:47.436
like, how good are you
with young children?

00:00:47.436 --> 00:00:49.716
Apparently, Retrievers
are really good.

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:53.856
Norwegian Lunderhunds
are not the best.

00:00:53.856 --> 00:00:55.416
Then there's the
trait description.

00:00:55.416 --> 00:00:58.356
I think this is a
pretty wild one.

00:00:58.356 --> 00:01:03.456
This is a just written
out description.

00:01:03.456 --> 00:01:05.916
And I actually love this like--

00:01:05.916 --> 00:01:08.706
so for affectionate with
family, if you get a five,

00:01:08.706 --> 00:01:14.166
it means you're lovey dovey,
which I find to be just great.

00:01:14.166 --> 00:01:16.026
Yeah, so Retriever's
lovey dovey.

00:01:16.026 --> 00:01:20.264
And then breed rank all.

00:01:20.264 --> 00:01:21.556
This is a little bit different.

00:01:21.556 --> 00:01:29.796
This is like from 2013 to 2020
or 2020 rankings of the dogs

00:01:29.796 --> 00:01:31.266
basically over time.

00:01:31.266 --> 00:01:35.226
What we can look at is maybe
comparing dogs across traits

00:01:35.226 --> 00:01:39.096
and working on a Shiny
app that lets you--

00:01:39.096 --> 00:01:41.646
because there are
a lot of dogs here

00:01:41.646 --> 00:01:43.246
if you look at the breed traits.

00:01:43.246 --> 00:01:45.276
So it'd be nice if
a user could choose

00:01:45.276 --> 00:01:48.096
the dogs they want
to look at and be

00:01:48.096 --> 00:01:50.316
able to compare them in a plot.

00:01:50.316 --> 00:01:51.779
There's a little
bit of wrangling.

00:01:51.779 --> 00:01:53.196
Here's a weird
thing, I can either

00:01:53.196 --> 00:01:56.076
wrangle it in siuba or pandas.

00:01:56.076 --> 00:02:00.976
For me, siuba is the most fun,
and I'm pretty used to it.

00:02:00.976 --> 00:02:04.226
So basically, this
is a port of--

00:02:04.226 --> 00:02:07.656
let me change from
dplyr to Python.

00:02:07.656 --> 00:02:10.416
And I've kind of come
to rely on it as a thing

00:02:10.416 --> 00:02:13.326
that just kind of
wraps pandas and does

00:02:13.326 --> 00:02:17.386
a little bit on top of pandas
to make life a little easier.

00:02:17.386 --> 00:02:21.426
So the way it works
is pretty similar

00:02:21.426 --> 00:02:24.036
to dplyr with a
couple of twists.

00:02:24.036 --> 00:02:29.826
So for example, if I want
to find a dog that's not

00:02:29.826 --> 00:02:33.226
very affectionate with family--

00:02:33.226 --> 00:02:35.226
these columns are crazy,
I may have to fix that.

00:02:35.226 --> 00:02:40.806
But I could do where it's one.

00:02:40.806 --> 00:02:43.326
So the Anatolian Shepherd
dogs, apparently, they're

00:02:43.326 --> 00:02:45.886
very independent.

00:02:45.886 --> 00:02:51.486
So the key with siuba
is that with dplyr, it

00:02:51.486 --> 00:02:54.306
would look pretty similar.

00:02:54.306 --> 00:03:00.786
You'd have a pipe and then
you'd have this just by itself,

00:03:00.786 --> 00:03:02.746
something like this.

00:03:02.746 --> 00:03:05.556
So the big differences
are now, the pipe's

00:03:05.556 --> 00:03:08.496
like a greater than
sign, and we're

00:03:08.496 --> 00:03:13.686
using an underscore rather
than referring to the columns

00:03:13.686 --> 00:03:15.921
directly.

00:03:15.921 --> 00:03:17.796
This is useful because
it's way easier for me

00:03:17.796 --> 00:03:22.309
to just keep thinking in
dplyr than to kind of switch.

00:03:22.309 --> 00:03:23.976
And it's not that
different from pandas.

00:03:23.976 --> 00:03:26.766
Pandas would be
like, breed traits,

00:03:26.766 --> 00:03:31.206
affectionate with
family, and then you

00:03:31.206 --> 00:03:32.496
do something like this.

00:03:32.496 --> 00:03:36.286
There are a few ways to do it.

00:03:36.286 --> 00:03:40.176
So it's kind of like there's
always like a pretty simple--

00:03:40.176 --> 00:03:42.006
the translations to
pandas aren't crazy,

00:03:42.006 --> 00:03:46.626
but it just cuts out
some writing basically.

00:03:46.626 --> 00:03:48.636
The other useful
things-- and I'm

00:03:48.636 --> 00:03:51.636
cheating because I
saw Dave with this.

00:03:51.636 --> 00:03:57.186
But for reshaping, it'd
be nice to tidy this

00:03:57.186 --> 00:04:03.036
so that a lot of
these columns become--

00:04:03.036 --> 00:04:04.416
to go from wider to longer.

00:04:04.416 --> 00:04:07.296
